    <h1 style="color:#0f1f3d;">Truck Batteries for the Saudi Arabia Market</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Saudi Arabia is a high-volume, high-heat commercial-vehicle market where Japanese and
      European heavy trucks run side by side. That means JIS N150/N200 batteries for Hino, Isuzu,
      Fuso and UD Trucks, and DIN88/DIN100 alongside them for European fleets — all specified for
      summer temperatures that routinely push past 40&deg;C.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:20px 0;padding:16px 20px;background:#f4f7fb;border-left:4px solid #f5a623;border-radius:6px;">
      <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.15rem;">Direct Answer</h2>
      <p style="margin:0;">Saudi Arabia's truck fleet is a mix of Japanese heavy trucks (Hino, Isuzu, Fuso, UD Trucks) running JIS N150/N200 batteries, and European trucks (Mercedes-Benz, Volvo, Scania, MAN, DAF, Iveco) that in this market are also commonly specified in the N150/N200 group, with DIN88/DIN100 as the European alternative. The dominant battery groups are N150 (145G51) and N200 (190H52), and the operating environment — summer maximums of 40&ndash;50&deg;C — is the single largest stress on service life. Imports clear through SASO/SABER conformity and the GCC G-Mark, with Jeddah Islamic Port and King Abdulaziz Port Dammam as the main entry points.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Market Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Saudi Arabia runs one of the largest heavy-duty truck fleets in the Gulf. Market research
      puts the Kingdom's heavy-duty truck market at roughly <strong>USD 9.6&nbsp;billion (2026)</strong>,
      with the major nameplates — Daimler Truck (Mercedes-Benz), Volvo Trucks, MAN, Isuzu and Hino —
      all active
      (<a href="https://www.mordorintelligence.com/industry-reports/saudi-arabia-heavy-duty-truck-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mordor Intelligence</a>).
      Japanese brands have deep, decades-long distribution: Hino has exported to the Kingdom since
      the 1970s through its Jamjoom Vehicles &amp; Equipment network
      (<a href="https://www.hino.com.sa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hino Saudi Arabia</a>),
      and Isuzu has been represented by Al Yemni Group since 1980
      (<a href="https://isuzu-intl.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Isuzu International</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      The precise split of the <em>installed</em> fleet by brand is not published in a single
      official source, so treat the following as the general structure rather than an exact
      proportion: a strong Japanese light-to-heavy truck base (Hino, Isuzu, Mitsubishi Fuso, UD
      Trucks) alongside a significant European long-haul segment (Mercedes-Benz, Volvo, Scania,
      MAN, DAF, Iveco). <em>Precise brand share: Not publicly verified.</em>
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Climate &amp; Duty Demands</h2>
    <p>
      Heat is the defining stress. Riyadh's mean daily maximum sits at 42.5&deg;C in June and
      43.5&ndash;43.6&deg;C through July and August, with a recorded high of 49.8&deg;C
      (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_of_Saudi_Arabia" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia — Climate of Saudi Arabia</a>;
      official data via the <a href="https://www.ncm.gov.sa/en/the-climate/climate-of-kingdom" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Center for Meteorology</a>).
      Summer maxima across the Kingdom typically run 35&ndash;45&deg;C, with occasional 50&deg;C in the
      eastern province
      (<a href="https://www.arabiaweather.com/en/content/saudi-arabia-is-approaching-the-peak-of-summer-heat-will-it-pass-it-without-recording-scorching-temperatures" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ArabiaWeather</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      For a lead-acid starting battery this is harder duty than cold. Elevated temperature
      accelerates plate ageing and increases electrolyte water loss in flooded designs, which
      shortens service life and raises self-discharge while a vehicle sits. Industry references
      describe cycle life falling roughly 20% per 10&deg;C rise in ambient temperature, dropping
      below ~200 cycles at 40&deg;C
      (<a href="https://www.everexceed.com/blog/the-impact-of-high-temperatures-on-lead-acid-batteries-and-recommended-solutions_b663" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EverExceed</a>;
      <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378775303009340" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Journal of Power Sources — lead-acid ageing</a>).
      The practical consequence for buyers: thick-plate flooded construction, correct electrolyte
      maintenance, or a sealed AGM option where the fleet prefers maintenance-free operation.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Vehicle Mix &amp; Common Battery Groups</h2>
    <p>
      The battery group follows the vehicle. On the Japanese side, Hino 700, Isuzu Giga, Fuso Super
      Great and UD Quon are specified in the JIS <strong>N150</strong> (and N200 where heavier
      cranking or a taller case is required). On the European side, Mercedes-Benz Actros/Atego,
      Volvo FH/FM, Scania R, MAN TGS/TGX, DAF XF and Iveco Stralis are also commonly listed in the
      N150/N200 group in Middle East fitment references, with the DIN88/DIN100 group as the
      alternative European code. The two groups that dominate Saudi fleets are therefore:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Battery group</th><th>DINWEYS model</th><th>Capacity / CCA</th><th>Typical vehicle class</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>JIS N150</td><td>145G51</td><td>135 Ah · 900 A (JIS)</td><td>Japanese &amp; European heavy trucks, buses</td></tr>
        <tr><td>JIS N200</td><td>190H52</td><td>200 Ah · 1100 A (JIS)</td><td>Heavy trucks, semi-trailers, high-load buses</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DIN88</td><td>58827</td><td>88 Ah · 800 A (EN)</td><td>European trucks (MAN, Scania, Volvo, Mercedes)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DIN100</td><td>60038</td><td>100 Ah · 870 A (EN)</td><td>European trucks (MAN, Scania, Volvo, Mercedes)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="data-note">
      Group-to-vehicle mapping is a third-party fitment reference (Club Assist / Century selection
      guides) — a compatible reference, not a DINWEYS OEM confirmation. Confirm dimensions, terminal
      layout and voltage against the specific truck before ordering.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Standards</h2>
    <p>
      Two standards run side by side. JIS N-series batteries (N150/N200) serve the Japanese and
      Korean truck base — and, in the Middle East, most European trucks are also specified in the
      N150/N200 group by independent fitment references. DIN/EN batteries in codes DIN88 and DIN100
      cover European vehicles where the original fitment uses that code. Match the battery to the
      vehicle's group, dimensions and terminal orientation rather than to the region alone. For a
      technical comparison of the standards, see
      <a href="/compare/jis-vs-din-vs-bci/" style="color:#1e5aa8;">JIS vs DIN vs BCI</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">DINWEYS Relevance</h2>
    <p>
      DINWEYS — manufactured by Chengguang Power Tech Co., Ltd. — carries the two group families
      that dominate the Saudi fleet in one line: JIS N150 (145G51) and N200 (190H52) for the
      Japanese truck base, and DIN88 (58827) and DIN100 (60038) for European vehicles. These are
      positioned as <strong>compatible references</strong> matched by group, dimensions and CCA —
      not as OEM replacements — so a distributor can cover a mixed Japanese/European fleet from a
      single manufacturer and a single shipment.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Buyer Considerations</h2>
    <ol class="takeaways-list">
      <li><strong>Match the battery to the vehicle, not the country.</strong> Confirm group size, physical dimensions, terminal orientation and system voltage (many Saudi heavy trucks are 24V — two 12V batteries in series) against the specific unit.</li>
      <li><strong>Budget for heat-shortened life.</strong> High ambient temperatures reduce service life, so a thick-plate flooded design or sealed AGM and a disciplined electrolyte/test schedule are worth more in Riyadh and Dammam than in temperate markets.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear conformity before shipment.</strong> SASO/SABER (PCoC + SCoC) and the GCC G-Mark gate customs clearance; confirm the current scheme and labelling with your importer.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan the port.</strong> Jeddah Islamic Port serves the western region on the Red Sea Asia-Europe lane; King Abdulaziz Port Dammam serves the eastern/Arabian Gulf side. Choose the entry point nearest your distribution centre.</li>
    </ol>

